\title{Sheet Numbers and Relative Trivialisations of Coverings}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{September 1, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a \emph{relative} theory of even coverings. For a map $f : E \to X$ of topological
spaces we study the \textbf{sheet number} $\sh_f(x) := \#\,f^{-1}(x)$, defined as a natural
number with the convention that empty and infinite fibres both receive the value $0$. Our first
group of results establishes the rigidity of this invariant on the locus where $f$ is a covering
map: being evenly covered with a fixed fibre is an open condition on the base point; consequently
the sheet number is locally constant on any set $S$ over which $f$ is a covering map, it is both
lower and upper semicontinuous at every point of $S$, it is continuous into the discrete natural
numbers, and it is constant whenever $S$ is preconnected. Running the same argument on the
proposition ``the fibre is nonempty'' rather than on its cardinality yields a \textbf{dichotomy}:
over a preconnected set on which $f$ is a covering map, either every fibre is empty or every
fibre is nonempty --- in particular, a covering over a connected base that hits one point is
surjective.

Our second and principal contribution is the \textbf{relative trivialisation theorem}. The
standard definition of an evenly covered point produces \emph{some} trivialising neighbourhood,
with no control over its location; in applications one has committed in advance to an open region
$U$ of the base and needs the trivialisation to live inside $U$. We introduce the notion of a
\textbf{sheet system} over a set $V \subseteq X$ indexed by a set $\iota$: a family of partial
homeomorphisms $\varphi_i : E \rightharpoonup X$ with open sources, all with target exactly $V$,
each agreeing with $f$ on its source, with pairwise disjoint sources whose union is $f^{-1}(V)$.
We prove that sheet systems restrict along open subsets of the base \emph{without changing the
index set}, that they can be reindexed along bijections, and that they can be built explicitly
from any abstract even covering. Combining these gives: if $f$ is a covering map on an open $U$
and $x \in U$, there exists an open $V$ with $x \in V \subseteq U$ carrying a sheet system indexed
by the fibre $f^{-1}(x)$. Conversely, a sheet system over an open $V$ exhibits every point of $V$
as evenly covered by its own fibre, with no topology on the index set required. We obtain a clean
characterisation: for open $U$, $f$ is a covering map on $U$ if and only if every point of $U$ has
an open neighbourhood inside $U$ over which $f$ admits a sheet system. We discuss the
specialisation to piecewise-linear and polyhedral (tropical) maps, where sheet systems are
supplied directly by the cell structure and the characterisation becomes a finite combinatorial
check.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} covering map, evenly covered neighbourhood, sheet number, local
trivialisation, semicontinuity, locally constant function, piecewise-linear map, tropical
geometry.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem}

Let $f : E \to X$ be a map of topological spaces. The simplest invariant attached to a point
$x \in X$ is the cardinality of its fibre $f^{-1}(x)$. In general this invariant is wildly
discontinuous: the squaring map $\mathbb{C} \to \mathbb{C}$ has a fibre of size $1$ over the origin
and of size $2$ everywhere else; a polynomial family acquires and loses roots as parameters vary;
a projection of a polyhedral complex has fibre counts that jump across walls.

The class of maps for which the fibre count is rigid is the class of \emph{covering maps}, and the
mechanism is entirely local.

\begin{definition}[Evenly covered point]\label{def:evenly}
Let $I$ be a topological space. A point $x \in X$ is \emph{evenly covered by $f$ with fibre $I$}
if there is an open set $U \subseteq X$ with $x \in U$ such that $f^{-1}(U)$ is open in $E$,
together with a homeomorphism
\[
  H : f^{-1}(U) \;\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\; U \times I
\]
satisfying $\pi_U \circ H = f$ on $f^{-1}(U)$, where $\pi_U$ is the first projection. In
applications $I$ is discrete.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Relative covering map]
For $S \subseteq X$, the map $f$ is a \emph{covering map on $S$} if every $x \in S$ is evenly
covered by $f$ with some discrete fibre.
\end{definition}

Two features of Definition~\ref{def:evenly} create friction in practice.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{The neighbourhood is not under our control.} The definition asserts existence of
\emph{some} $U$. If a construction or a hypothesis is only valid on a prescribed open region
$U_0$, the trivialising $U$ may spill outside $U_0$.
\item \textbf{The trivialisation is an abstract homeomorphism.} For gluing, for computation, and
for combinatorial models, one wants a concrete indexed list of open pieces upstairs, each equipped
with a genuine inverse map, rather than a single opaque isomorphism onto a product.
\end{enumerate}

The purpose of this paper is to remove both frictions, and to record the rigidity consequences of
the local picture in the precise forms in which they are used.

\subsection{Conventions}

All maps are between topological spaces; no separation, local connectedness, or local compactness
hypotheses are imposed. ``Preconnected'' means the set cannot be separated by two open sets meeting
it in disjoint nonempty pieces covering it; unlike ``connected'', a preconnected set may be empty.
A \emph{partial homeomorphism} $\varphi : E \rightharpoonup X$ consists of an open \emph{source}
$\Sigma \subseteq E$, an open \emph{target} $T \subseteq X$, and mutually inverse continuous
bijections between them.

The \textbf{cardinality convention} matters. We define $\sh_f(x)$ to be the natural number
$\#\,f^{-1}(x)$, where an infinite set is assigned the value $0$. Thus $\sh_f(x) = 0$ means ``the
fibre is empty or infinite''. This makes the invariant $\mathbb{N}$-valued and hence available for
semicontinuity statements; the loss of information at $0$ is exactly compensated by the dichotomy
of Theorem~\ref{thm:dichotomy}, which tracks emptiness separately.

\section{The sheet number and its rigidity}

\begin{definition}[Sheet number]
For $f : E \to X$ and $x \in X$, $\sh_f(x) := \#\, f^{-1}(x) \in \mathbb{N}$, with the convention
above.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Fibres of an even covering]\label{lem:fibres}
If $x$ is evenly covered with fibre $I$, via $H : f^{-1}(U) \cong U \times I$, then for every
$y \in U$ the map $H$ restricts to a bijection $f^{-1}(y) \cong \{y\} \times I \cong I$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Since $\pi_U \circ H = f$, a point $e \in f^{-1}(U)$ lies in $f^{-1}(y)$ exactly when
$H(e) \in \{y\}\times I$; and $f^{-1}(y) \subseteq f^{-1}(U)$ because $y \in U$. As $H$ is a
bijection onto $U \times I$, it restricts to a bijection $f^{-1}(y) \to \{y\}\times I$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Even covering is an open condition]\label{thm:open-condition}
If $x$ is evenly covered by $f$ with fibre $I$, then every point of some open neighbourhood of $x$
is evenly covered by $f$ with the \emph{same} fibre $I$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $U, H$ witness even covering at $x$. For any $y \in U$ the same data $U, H$ witness even
covering at $y$: the only clause of Definition~\ref{def:evenly} mentioning the point is
``$x \in U$'', and $y \in U$ holds by assumption. Since $U$ is open and contains $x$, this is a
statement about a neighbourhood of $x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:count}
If $x$ is evenly covered with fibre $I$, then $\sh_f(x) = \# I$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:nonempty}
If $x$ is evenly covered with fibre $I$, then $f^{-1}(x) \neq \emptyset$ if and only if
$I \neq \emptyset$. Explicitly: given $e \in f^{-1}(x)$ the second component of $H(e)$ is an
element of $I$; given $i \in I$ the point $H^{-1}(x,i)$ lies in $f^{-1}(x)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Local invariance]\label{prop:localinv}
If $x$ is evenly covered with fibre $I$, then on a neighbourhood of $x$ we have
(i) $\sh_f(y) = \sh_f(x)$, and (ii) $f^{-1}(y) \neq \emptyset \iff f^{-1}(x) \neq \emptyset$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:open-condition} there is a neighbourhood on each point of which $f$ is evenly
covered with fibre $I$. Apply Corollary~\ref{cor:count} and Corollary~\ref{cor:nonempty} there and
at $x$.
\end{proof}

Fix $S \subseteq X$ and assume $f$ is a covering map on $S$.

\begin{theorem}[Local constancy]\label{thm:locconst}
Let $f$ be a covering map on $S$ and $x \in S$. Then $\sh_f(y) = \sh_f(x)$ for all $y$ in a
neighbourhood of $x$ in $X$. Consequently $\sh_f|_S : S \to \mathbb{N}$ is locally constant.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The first claim is Proposition~\ref{prop:localinv}(i) applied to the even covering at $x$. For the
second, the inclusion $S \hookrightarrow X$ is continuous, so eventual equality along the
neighbourhood filter of $x$ in $X$ pulls back to eventual equality in $S$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Constancy on preconnected sets]\label{thm:const}
Let $f$ be a covering map on a preconnected set $S$. Then $\sh_f(x) = \sh_f(y)$ for all
$x, y \in S$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$S$ is a preconnected space and $\sh_f|_S$ is locally constant by Theorem~\ref{thm:locconst}. A
locally constant function on a preconnected space is constant: the preimage of a value and the
preimage of its complement are both open and partition $S$, so one is empty. Apply this to the
value $\sh_f(x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Lower semicontinuity]\label{thm:lsc}
Let $f$ be a covering map on $S$ and $x \in S$. For every $n < \sh_f(x)$ one has $n < \sh_f(y)$
for all $y$ in a neighbourhood of $x$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Upper semicontinuity]\label{thm:usc}
Under the same hypotheses, for every $n > \sh_f(x)$ one has $n > \sh_f(y)$ for all $y$ in a
neighbourhood of $x$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Continuity]\label{thm:cont}
Under the same hypotheses, $\sh_f$ is continuous at $x$, where $\mathbb{N}$ carries the discrete
topology.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorems \ref{thm:lsc}--\ref{thm:cont}]
All three follow from Theorem~\ref{thm:locconst}. Take the neighbourhood on which
$\sh_f(y) = \sh_f(x)$ and rewrite the strict inequality (for \ref{thm:lsc} and \ref{thm:usc}). For
\ref{thm:cont}, the constant map at $\sh_f(x)$ converges to $\sh_f(x)$ and agrees with $\sh_f$ on a
neighbourhood, so the two have the same limit along the neighbourhood filter of $x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Constancy implies all three, so as theorems about the covering locus they carry no extra content.
Their value is that they are the \emph{stable} half of the phenomenon and remain meaningful where
constancy fails: fibre counts of algebraic maps are lower semicontinuous under perturbation (roots
split), while fibre counts of proper maps are upper semicontinuous (roots collide but do not appear
from nowhere). The theorems above delimit precisely the region where both hold.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Fibre dichotomy]\label{thm:dichotomy}
Let $f$ be a covering map on a preconnected set $S$. Then either $f^{-1}(x) = \emptyset$ for every
$x \in S$, or $f^{-1}(x) \neq \emptyset$ for every $x \in S$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Consider $P : S \to \{\text{true},\text{false}\}$, $P(y) := \big(f^{-1}(y) \neq \emptyset\big)$,
with discrete codomain. By Proposition~\ref{prop:localinv}(ii), $P$ is constant on a neighbourhood
of each point of $S$ --- on a trivialising neighbourhood its value equals ``$I \neq \emptyset$'',
independently of the point. So $P$ is locally constant on the preconnected space $S$, hence
constant. If $S = \emptyset$ the first alternative holds vacuously; otherwise pick $x_0 \in S$ and
read off which alternative $P(x_0)$ selects.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[All-or-nothing surjectivity]
If $f$ is a covering map on a connected set $S$ and $f^{-1}(x_0) \neq \emptyset$ for some
$x_0 \in S$, then $S \subseteq f(E)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}[Sharpness]
Preconnectedness cannot be dropped: with $X = \{0,1\}$ discrete, $E = \{\ast\}$ and $f(\ast) = 0$,
the map $f$ is a covering map on $X$, $\sh_f(0) = 1$, $\sh_f(1) = 0$, and neither alternative of
Theorem~\ref{thm:dichotomy} holds on $X$. Both theorems hold on each component separately.
\end{remark}

\section{Sheet systems}

\begin{definition}[Sheet system]\label{def:sheetsystem}
Let $f : E \to X$, let $V \subseteq X$, and let $\iota$ be a set. A \textbf{sheet system} for $f$
over $V$ indexed by $\iota$ consists of a family $(\varphi_i)_{i \in \iota}$ of partial
homeomorphisms $E \rightharpoonup X$, with sources $\Sigma_i$ (open in $E$) and targets $T_i$ (open
in $X$), such that
\begin{itemize}
\item[(S1)] \emph{Common target:} $T_i = V$ for every $i \in \iota$;
\item[(S2)] \emph{Restriction of $f$:} $\varphi_i(e) = f(e)$ for every $i$ and every
$e \in \Sigma_i$;
\item[(S3)] \emph{Disjointness:} $\Sigma_i \cap \Sigma_j = \emptyset$ whenever $i \neq j$;
\item[(S4)] \emph{Exhaustion:} $\bigcup_{i \in \iota} \Sigma_i = f^{-1}(V)$.
\end{itemize}
We call $\Sigma_i$ the $i$-th \emph{sheet} and $\varphi_i^{-1} : V \to \Sigma_i$ the $i$-th
\emph{section}.
\end{definition}

Axiom (S1) forces $V$ open whenever $\iota \neq \emptyset$; when $\iota = \emptyset$, axiom (S4)
forces $f^{-1}(V) = \emptyset$. Where openness of $V$ is needed we assume it, covering the
empty-index case uniformly. The index $\iota$ is a bare set, carrying no topology.

\begin{example}
(a) For $f(t) = e^{2\pi i t}$ from $\mathbb{R}$ to the unit circle and $V$ the arc
$\{e^{2\pi i \theta} : \theta \in (0,1)\}$, the sheets are $\Sigma_n = (n, n+1)$, $n \in \mathbb{Z}$,
with $\varphi_n^{-1}(e^{2\pi i\theta}) = n + \theta$. (b) For $f(z) = z^n$ on the unit circle and
$V$ a proper open arc, the sheets are the $n$ preimage arcs. (c) For a piecewise-affine
$f : \mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ and $V$ an open interval containing no critical value, the sheets are
the intersections with $f^{-1}(V)$ of the maximal open intervals of affinity.
\end{example}

Let $(\varphi_i)_{i\in\iota}$ be a sheet system for $f$ over $V$.

\begin{proposition}[Injectivity on sheets]\label{prop:inj}
For each $i$, $f$ is injective on $\Sigma_i$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
If $a,b \in \Sigma_i$ with $f(a) = f(b)$, then by (S2) $\varphi_i(a) = \varphi_i(b)$, and
$\varphi_i$ is injective on its source.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Surjectivity onto the base]\label{prop:surj}
For each $i$ and $y \in V$, the point $\varphi_i^{-1}(y)$ lies in $\Sigma_i$ and satisfies
$f(\varphi_i^{-1}(y)) = y$. Hence $f(\Sigma_i) = V$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
By (S1), $y$ is in the target of $\varphi_i$, so $e := \varphi_i^{-1}(y) \in \Sigma_i$; by (S2) and
the inverse property, $f(e) = \varphi_i(e) = y$.
\end{proof}

Thus for each $y \in V$ the map $i \mapsto \varphi_i^{-1}(y)$ is a bijection $\iota \to f^{-1}(y)$:
it lands in the fibre by Proposition~\ref{prop:surj}, is injective by (S3), and is surjective by
(S4) with Proposition~\ref{prop:inj}.

\begin{proposition}[Sheetwise detection of openness]\label{prop:opendetect}
Fix $i \in \iota$ and let $W \subseteq V$. Then $W$ is open in $X$ if and only if
$f^{-1}(W) \cap \Sigma_i$ is open in $E$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
By (S1), $W$ lies in the target of $\varphi_i$. The key identity is
$\varphi_i^{-1}(W) = f^{-1}(W) \cap \Sigma_i$: the image of $W$ under the inverse chart is
$\Sigma_i \cap \varphi_i^{-1}(W)$, and on $\Sigma_i$ we have $\varphi_i = f$ by (S2). Since
$\varphi_i$ is a homeomorphism from the open set $\Sigma_i$ onto the open set $V$, a subset of $V$
is open exactly when its inverse image in $\Sigma_i$ is open.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[A sheet system exhibits even coverings]\label{thm:sheets-even}
Suppose $V$ is open and $f$ admits a sheet system over $V$ indexed by $\iota$; give $\iota$ the
discrete topology. Then every $x \in V$ is evenly covered by $f$ with fibre $\iota$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $\iota = \emptyset$ then $f^{-1}(V) = \emptyset$ by (S4), and $U := V$ with the empty
homeomorphism $\emptyset \cong V \times \emptyset$ witnesses even covering.

Otherwise define $H : f^{-1}(V) \to V \times \iota$ by $H(e) = (f(e), i)$ where $i$ is the unique
index with $e \in \Sigma_i$ (existence and uniqueness are (S4) and (S3)); its inverse is
$(y,i) \mapsto \varphi_i^{-1}(y)$, well defined by Proposition~\ref{prop:surj}. Compatibility with
$f$ is immediate. That $H$ is a homeomorphism follows from: the $\Sigma_i$ are open, disjoint and
exhaust $f^{-1}(V)$ (so $f^{-1}(V)$ is open and its topology is determined sheetwise); $f$ is
injective on each sheet (Proposition~\ref{prop:inj}) and maps it onto $V$
(Proposition~\ref{prop:surj}); and openness is detected sheetwise
(Proposition~\ref{prop:opendetect}). These are exactly the hypotheses of the standard construction
of a trivialisation with discrete fibre from a disjoint open decomposition of the preimage.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Intrinsic indexing]\label{thm:intrinsic}
Suppose $V$ is open and $f$ admits a sheet system over $V$ indexed by \emph{any} set $\iota$. Then
every $x \in V$ is evenly covered by $f$ with fibre $f^{-1}(x)$, given the discrete topology.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Equip $\iota$ with the discrete topology and apply Theorem~\ref{thm:sheets-even}. By
Lemma~\ref{lem:fibres} the fibre $f^{-1}(x)$ is in bijection with $\iota$, and the bijection is a
homeomorphism since both sides are discrete ($f^{-1}(x)$ is identified with $\{x\}\times\iota$).
Transport the trivialisation along it.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:sheetnumber}
If $V$ is open and $f$ admits a sheet system over $V$ indexed by $\iota$, then $\sh_f(x) = \#\iota$
for all $x \in V$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Construction, restriction, reindexing}

\begin{construction}[Sheet charts of an even covering]\label{con:charts}
Let $U \subseteq X$ be open with $f^{-1}(U)$ open, let $I$ be discrete, and let
$H : f^{-1}(U) \to U \times I$ be a homeomorphism with $\pi_U \circ H = f$. For $i \in I$ set
\[
  \Sigma_i := \{ e \in f^{-1}(U) : \pi_I(H(e)) = i \},\qquad
  \varphi_i := f|_{\Sigma_i},\qquad
  \varphi_i^{-1}(y) := H^{-1}(y,i)\ \ (y \in U).
\]
\end{construction}

\begin{theorem}[Existence of sheet systems at evenly covered points]\label{thm:exists-sheets}
With the data of Construction~\ref{con:charts}, $(\varphi_i)_{i \in I}$ is a sheet system for $f$
over $U$ indexed by $I$. Consequently, if $x$ is evenly covered with fibre $I$, there is an open
$V \ni x$ over which $f$ admits a sheet system indexed by $I$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Each $\Sigma_i$ is the preimage under the continuous $\pi_I \circ H$ of the open singleton $\{i\}$
(here discreteness of $I$ is used), inside the open set $f^{-1}(U)$; hence $\Sigma_i$ is open in
$E$. For $e \in \Sigma_i$ we have $H(e) = (f(e), i)$, so $H^{-1}(f(e),i) = e$; and for $y \in U$,
$f(H^{-1}(y,i)) = \pi_U(H(H^{-1}(y,i))) = y$. Thus $\varphi_i$ and $y \mapsto H^{-1}(y,i)$ are
mutually inverse between $\Sigma_i$ and $U$, giving (S1). Continuity of $\varphi_i$ holds because on
$f^{-1}(U)$ it equals $\pi_U \circ H$; continuity of the inverse because it is $H^{-1}(\cdot, i)$
followed by an inclusion. (S2) holds by construction. (S3): $e \in \Sigma_i \cap \Sigma_j$ forces
$i = \pi_I(H(e)) = j$. (S4): every $e \in f^{-1}(U)$ lies in $\Sigma_{\pi_I(H(e))}$, and each
$\Sigma_i \subseteq f^{-1}(U)$. Finally, if $I = \emptyset$ then $f^{-1}(U) = \emptyset$ and the
empty family is a sheet system over $U$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Restriction of sheet systems]\label{thm:restrict}
Let $(\varphi_i)_{i\in\iota}$ be a sheet system for $f$ over $V$ and let $W \subseteq X$ be open.
Let $\psi_i$ be the composite of $\varphi_i$ with the identity partial homeomorphism of $W$;
explicitly, $\psi_i$ is $\varphi_i$ with source $\Sigma_i \cap f^{-1}(W)$ and target $W \cap V$.
Then $(\psi_i)_{i\in\iota}$ is a sheet system for $f$ over $W \cap V$, indexed by the \emph{same}
set $\iota$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $\Sigma_i' := \operatorname{dom}(\psi_i)$. Composition of partial homeomorphisms is a partial
homeomorphism.

\emph{Source identification.} $\Sigma_i' = \Sigma_i \cap \varphi_i^{-1}(W) = \Sigma_i \cap
f^{-1}(W)$, since $\varphi_i = f$ on $\Sigma_i$ by (S2).

\emph{(S1).} The target is $W \cap T_i = W \cap V$ by (S1) for $\varphi_i$.

\emph{(S2).} For $e \in \Sigma_i'$, $\psi_i(e) = \varphi_i(e) = f(e)$.

\emph{(S3).} $\Sigma_i' \subseteq \Sigma_i$, so disjointness is inherited.

\emph{(S4).} Using distributivity,
\[
  \bigcup_i \Sigma_i' = \Big(\bigcup_i \Sigma_i\Big)\cap f^{-1}(W)
  = f^{-1}(V) \cap f^{-1}(W) = f^{-1}(W \cap V). \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

Two features deserve emphasis. First, \emph{the index set is unchanged}: shrinking the base neither
loses nor duplicates sheets, so restriction is composable and the sheet count is stable under
shrinking, as it must be by Theorem~\ref{thm:locconst}. Second, only openness of $W$ is used, and
only to make the identity of $W$ a partial homeomorphism.

\begin{lemma}[Reindexing]\label{lem:reindex}
Let $(\varphi_i)_{i\in\iota}$ be a sheet system for $f$ over $V$ and $\sigma : \kappa \to \iota$ a
bijection. Then $(\varphi_{\sigma(k)})_{k\in\kappa}$ is a sheet system for $f$ over $V$ indexed by
$\kappa$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
(S1), (S2) are pointwise and inherited. (S3) follows from injectivity of $\sigma$, (S4) from its
surjectivity.
\end{proof}

\section{The relative trivialisation theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Relative trivialisation]\label{thm:relative}
Let $U \subseteq X$ be open and suppose $f$ is a covering map on $U$. Then for every $x \in U$
there is an open set $V$ with $x \in V \subseteq U$ such that $f$ admits a sheet system over $V$
indexed by the fibre $f^{-1}(x)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By hypothesis $x$ is evenly covered with some discrete fibre $I$. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:exists-sheets} there is an open $V_0 \ni x$ and a sheet system for $f$ over $V_0$
indexed by $I$. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:restrict} with $W := U$: we obtain a sheet system over
$U \cap V_0$, still indexed by $I$. Set $V := U \cap V_0$; then $x \in V \subseteq U$ and $V$ is
open. Finally Lemma~\ref{lem:fibres} gives a bijection $f^{-1}(x) \cong I$, along which
Lemma~\ref{lem:reindex} reindexes the sheet system.
\end{proof}

The indexing by $f^{-1}(x)$ is the geometrically correct one: sheets over $V$ correspond one-to-one
with points above $x$, the $i$-th sheet containing exactly the point $\varphi_i^{-1}(x)$.

\begin{theorem}[Characterisation of relative covering maps]\label{thm:charac}
Let $U \subseteq X$ be open. Then $f$ is a covering map on $U$ if and only if for every $x \in U$
there exist a set $\kappa$ and an open $V$ with $x \in V \subseteq U$ such that $f$ admits a sheet
system over $V$ indexed by $\kappa$. No topology on $\kappa$ is required in either direction.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) Theorem~\ref{thm:relative}, with $\kappa := f^{-1}(x)$.
($\Leftarrow$) Given $x \in U$ take $V, \kappa$ as supplied; since $V$ is open and $x \in V$,
Theorem~\ref{thm:intrinsic} shows $x$ is evenly covered with discrete fibre $f^{-1}(x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:global}
If $U$ is open and $f$ admits a sheet system over $U$ indexed by $\iota$, then $f$ is a covering map
on $U$ and $\sh_f(x) = \#\iota$ for every $x \in U$; if $\iota \neq \emptyset$ then
$U \subseteq f(E)$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The piecewise-linear and polyhedral setting}

\subsection{Piecewise-affine maps of the line}

Let $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ be continuous and piecewise affine with breakpoints
$b_1 < \cdots < b_m$, affine with slope $s_k \neq 0$ on each open piece $P_k$. Let
$U := \mathbb{R}\setminus f(\{b_1,\dots,b_m\})$, the complement of the finite set of critical
values, and let $V$ be a connected component of $U$.

Over $V$, the sheets are $\Sigma_k := P_k \cap f^{-1}(V)$ for those pieces whose image meets $V$.
Each is an open interval on which $f$ is affine with nonzero slope, hence a homeomorphism onto $V$
(no critical value lies in $V$, so $f$ can neither turn around inside $\Sigma_k$ nor stop short);
the $\Sigma_k$ are disjoint; and they exhaust $f^{-1}(V)$ because a preimage point at a breakpoint
would force its image to be a critical value. Thus $(f|_{\Sigma_k})_k$ is a sheet system over $V$
and Corollary~\ref{cor:global} applies: $f$ is a covering over $V$ with constant sheet number equal
to the number of participating pieces, and either $f$ misses $V$ entirely or it hits every point of
$V$.

\subsection{Polyhedral maps}

Let $\Pi \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ and $\Delta \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$ be polyhedral complexes and
$f : \Pi \to \Delta$ affine on each cell. Fix a chamber $\sigma$ of $\Delta$ and set
$V := \relint(\sigma)$. Suppose every cell $\tau$ of $\Pi$ with $f(\tau) = \sigma$ and
$\dim\tau = \dim\sigma$ maps affinely and bijectively onto $\sigma$, and no lower-dimensional cell
of $\Pi$ meets $f^{-1}(V)$. Then the relative interiors of these $\tau$, with the restrictions of
$f$, form a sheet system over $V$, whose sections are the inverse affine maps. The sheet number over
$V$ is the number of such cells; Theorem~\ref{thm:charac} certifies the covering property, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:dichotomy} gives all-or-nothing surjectivity over the chamber. The relative form is
essential: the region of interest is dictated by the combinatorics, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:restrict} is what makes the trivialisation respect it.

\subsection{Algorithms}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Fibre enumeration.} Given piecewise-affine $f$ and a point $y$, solve
$f|_{P_k}(e) = y$ on each piece and retain solutions lying in $P_k$; this computes $f^{-1}(y)$ and
$\sh_f(y)$ in time linear in the number of pieces.
\item \textbf{Sheet system extraction.} Given a region $V$ free of critical values, output the
sheets $\Sigma_k = P_k \cap f^{-1}(V)$ with their sections; validating (S1)--(S4) reduces to
checking injectivity and surjectivity of each affine restriction and that no breakpoint maps into
$V$. Linear in the number of pieces.
\item \textbf{Restriction.} Given a sheet system over $V$ and open $W$, intersect each source with
$f^{-1}(W)$ and each target with $W$. Linear in the number of sheets, with no recomputation of
charts; this is Theorem~\ref{thm:restrict} in code, and preservation of the index set is its
practical content.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the relative form buys}

The abstract definition of an evenly covered point is an existence statement about a neighbourhood.
Three deficiencies follow, each addressed above: \emph{localisation} (Theorems~\ref{thm:restrict}
and \ref{thm:relative} put the trivialisation inside a prescribed region), \emph{concreteness}
(sheet systems name the pieces and their sections), and \emph{index bookkeeping}
(Theorem~\ref{thm:intrinsic} and Lemma~\ref{lem:reindex} show the index may be taken to be the fibre
itself, with no topology assumed in the input).

\subsection{On the cardinality convention}

Setting $\#(\text{infinite}) = 0$ makes semicontinuity expressible without extended arithmetic, at
the cost of conflating ``empty'' with ``infinite''. The conflation is harmless here: the dichotomy
tracks emptiness independently of cardinality and applies verbatim to infinite fibres; over regions
with finite fibres --- the polyhedral case --- the invariant is the honest count; and constancy
holds regardless, since both the cardinality and the emptiness of a fibre are transported by the
bijection of Lemma~\ref{lem:fibres}.

\subsection{Limitations}

Theorems~\ref{thm:relative} and \ref{thm:charac} require $U$ open: sheet systems have open targets,
so trivialisations over non-open regions are not expressible in Definition~\ref{def:sheetsystem}.
Nothing bounds the number of sheets, so under our convention $\sh_f \equiv 0$ for infinite-sheeted
coverings while the dichotomy remains informative. Finally, sheet systems are local objects; the
comparison of sheet systems over overlapping regions --- the transition data giving monodromy --- is
not developed here.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Gluing and monodromy.} Restriction puts sheet systems over $V_1$ and $V_2$ onto
$V_1 \cap V_2$ with their original index sets; on each component of the overlap the resulting
bijection of index sets is locally constant, yielding transition permutations. Formalising this
cocycle would give the monodromy representation and a classification of coverings by atlases of
sheet systems.
\item \textbf{Path and homotopy lifting.} The relative trivialisation theorem furnishes exactly the
data used in the Lebesgue-number proof of path lifting. Deriving unique path lifting and the
homotopy lifting property directly from atlases of sheet systems --- over an open subset of the
base, so that lifting is proved only where the covering hypothesis holds --- is the natural next
step.
\item \textbf{Degree theory for polyhedral maps.} Combining the constancy of the sheet number on
chambers with orientation data should give a combinatorial degree, computed as a signed sheet count,
together with a wall-crossing formula across the discriminant.
\item \textbf{Tropical applications.} Tropical morphisms are piecewise integral-affine, and
balancing relates local sheet counts across walls. Expressing the tropical degree as the constant
sheet number over a chamber, and deriving balancing as a compatibility of restricted sheet systems,
would place tropical multiplicities in this framework.
\item \textbf{Non-open base regions.} Extend the theory to locally closed $S$ by allowing charts
with targets open in $S$, and determine which results survive.
\item \textbf{Cardinal-valued sheet numbers.} Replace $\sh_f$ by a cardinal-valued invariant to
remove the empty/infinite conflation, and re-derive the semicontinuity package in the order topology
on cardinals.
\item \textbf{Effective certification.} Turn the pipeline above into a certificate format: a list of
sheets with explicit sections plus disjointness and exhaustion witnesses, constituting a checkable
proof that a map is a covering over a given region.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Summary of results}

\begin{longtable}{p{0.32\textwidth}p{0.6\textwidth}}
\toprule
\textbf{Result} & \textbf{Statement} \\
\midrule
Openness of even covering & If $x$ is evenly covered with fibre $I$, so is every point of a
neighbourhood, with the same $I$. \\
Fibre count & $\sh_f(x) = \#I$, and $f^{-1}(x) \neq \emptyset \iff I \neq \emptyset$. \\
Local constancy & $\sh_f$ is locally constant on any set over which $f$ is a covering map. \\
Constancy & $\sh_f$ is constant on a preconnected covering locus. \\
Semicontinuity & $\sh_f$ is lower and upper semicontinuous at each point of the covering locus,
hence continuous into discrete $\mathbb{N}$. \\
Dichotomy & Over a preconnected covering locus, either all fibres are empty or all are nonempty. \\
Sheet system basics & $f$ is injective on each sheet, maps each sheet onto $V$, and openness of
$W \subseteq V$ is detected on any single sheet. \\
Even covering from sheets & A sheet system over an open $V$ makes every point of $V$ evenly covered
--- by the index set, and intrinsically by its own fibre. \\
Restriction & A sheet system over $V$ restricts along open $W$ to one over $W \cap V$ with the same
index set. \\
Reindexing & Sheet systems transport along bijections of index sets. \\
Relative trivialisation & For open $U$ on which $f$ is a covering map and $x \in U$: there is an open
$V$ with $x \in V \subseteq U$ carrying a sheet system indexed by $f^{-1}(x)$. \\
Characterisation & For open $U$: $f$ is a covering map on $U$ iff every point of $U$ has an open
neighbourhood inside $U$ over which $f$ admits a sheet system. \\
\bottomrule
\end{longtable}

\end{document}

